        <body>
            <div xml:id="d207">

                <pb facs="0296" n="176" sameAs="frus:frus1905/pg176"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Chargé Coolidge to the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1905-04-26"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">April 26, 1905</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1870.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to transmit herewith
                    translations of two Imperial edicts which appeared in the Peking Gazette of
                    April 24 and 25, by which the more cruel forms of punishment now in use are
                    abolished or modified.</p>
                <p>These edicts were in response to a memorial of His Excellency Wu T’ing-fang. Mr.
                    Williams’s explanatory note is appended.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Gardner
                    Coolidge.</hi></signed></closer>




